{"hub":"occlusion","title":"Occlusion Final Hub","stateKey":"occl-state-v1","exams":[{"id":"midterm","label":"Midterm","date":"2026-10-21","time":"18:30","closes":"19:45"},{"id":"final","label":"Final","date":"2026-11-16","time":"18:30","closes":"19:45"}],"examInfo":[["What","The lectures from 10/22 on: L6, L7, L8 and L9 so far, with more added here as they are taught. The final is not comprehensive, but it builds on the midterm material."],["When","Monday, November 16, 6:30 pm, the same time window as the midterm: it closes at 7:45 pm. Moved up from Thursday, November 19. The format hasn't been announced yet; this card will change when it is."],["So far","L6, Occlusal Instrumentation (10/22): the three kinds of articulator, the facebow, CR, MIP and eccentric records, and how to choose an articulator. L7, Malocclusion and Dysfunction (10/29): functional disturbances and TMD, the five etiologies, splints and occlusal therapy, mobility, wear and bruxism. L8, Denture Occlusion (11/5): why a denture's occlusion is about keeping it from tipping, support, stability and retention, resorption, balanced, non-balanced and lingualized occlusion, and denture teeth. L9, Noncarious Cervical Lesions (Dr. Lawson, prerecorded for 11/12): carious vs noncarious, abrasion, abfraction and erosion, pH vs buffering capacity, and when to restore."],["Midterm lectures","L1-L5 stay in the hub for review. The final builds on them, so the terms (CR, CO, MIP), guidance and the determinants still matter."]],"courseDates":[{"date":"2026-09-17","label":"L1 Review of Basic Occlusion"},{"date":"2026-09-24","label":"L2 Functional TMJ Anatomy and L3 Mastication, Deglutition and Speech"},{"date":"2026-10-01","label":"L4 Occlusal Concepts and L5 Applying Occlusion"},{"date":"2026-10-21","label":"Midterm: L1-L5, 6:30 pm","kind":"exam"},{"date":"2026-10-22","label":"L6 Occlusal Instrumentation"},{"date":"2026-10-29","label":"L7 Malocclusion and Dysfunction"},{"date":"2026-11-05","label":"L8 Denture Occlusion"},{"date":"2026-11-12","label":"L9 Noncarious Cervical Lesions (Dr. Lawson, prerecorded)"},{"date":"2026-11-16","label":"Final: the lectures from 10/22 on, 6:30 pm","kind":"exam"}],"lectures":[{"id":"review","num":1,"title":"Review of Basic Occlusion","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"blue","source":"Occ Lec1 Review (slides + handout) + 9/17 recording + \"What cusp hits where\" help sheet","status":"taught","exam":"midterm"},{"id":"anatomy","num":2,"title":"Functional TMJ Anatomy and the Muscles of Mastication","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"red","source":"Occ Lec2-1 Anatomy (slides + handout) + 9/24 recording","status":"taught","exam":"midterm"},{"id":"function","num":3,"title":"Mastication, Deglutition and Speech","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"teal","source":"Occ Lec2-2 (slides + handout) + 9/24 recording","status":"taught","exam":"midterm"},{"id":"concepts","num":4,"title":"Occlusal Concepts: How We Got Here","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"violet","source":"Occ Lec3-1 Occlusal Concepts (slides + handout) + 10/1 recording","status":"taught","exam":"midterm"},{"id":"applying","num":5,"title":"Applying Occlusion: Operative, Fixed and Implants","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"amber","source":"Occ Lec3-2 Occlusal Correlations (slides + handout) + 10/1 recording","status":"taught","exam":"midterm"},{"id":"instruments","num":6,"title":"Occlusal Instrumentation: Articulators, Facebows and Records","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"green","source":"Occ Lec4 Occlusal Instrumentation (slides + handout) + 10/22 recording","status":"taught","exam":"final"},{"id":"malocclusion","num":7,"title":"Malocclusion and Dysfunction: Functional Disturbances and Etiologies","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"rose","source":"Occ Lec6 Malocclusion and Dysfunction (slides + handout) + 10/29 recording","status":"taught","exam":"final"},{"id":"dentures","num":8,"title":"Denture Occlusion: Balanced, Non-balanced and Lingualized","who":"Dr. Givan","tag":"sage","source":"Occ Lec7 RDP Occlusion (slides + handout) + 11/5 recording","status":"taught","exam":"final"},{"id":"nccl","num":9,"title":"Noncarious Cervical Lesions: Abrasion, Abfraction and Erosion","who":"Dr. Lawson","tag":"slate","source":"Dr. Lawson's prerecorded lecture for the 11/12 session (recording; no slides yet)","status":"taught","badge":"Recording","exam":"final"}],"likelyTopics":[{"lec":"instruments","t":"Articulator size: best value when it is similar in size to the patient; a small hinge costs chair time","basis":"hint","ids":["in-tf-9","in-zirconia","in-110","in-4inch","cs-in-crown-lab"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"Non-adjustable articulators: accurate only in MICP, an advantage to the lab, not to you","basis":"hint","ids":["in-nonadj-acc","in-nonadj-who","in-nonadj-record","in-2s-1"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"Choosing the articulator: anterior teeth need at least a semi-adjustable; the job dictates the tool","basis":"hint","ids":["in-ag-nonadj","in-sel-strong","in-sel-poor","in-job","in-num-5"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"The facebow mounts the maxillary cast only, on a three-point reference plane","basis":"hint","ids":["in-num-1","in-fb-points","in-frankfort"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"An arbitrary facebow is close enough (2-5 mm); skipping it risks a cant and mounting errors","basis":"hint","ids":["in-nofb","cs-in-rehab-fb","in-mt-post","in-tf-7"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"The MIP record: most common, most misused; hand articulate whenever you can","basis":"hint","ids":["in-mip-misused","in-lab-errors","in-hand-artic","cs-in-high","cs-in-high-fix"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"VPS MIP records go over the prep only, trimmed off cusp tips and soft tissue","basis":"hint","ids":["in-vps-fit","in-2s-7"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"Protrusive records are preferred for the SCI: a lateral record reads steeper","basis":"hint","ids":["in-why-prot","in-lat-sets","in-2s-3","in-tf-5","cs-in-rehab-sci"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"Eccentric records at 3-5 mm: farther out, the Bennett angle reads smaller","basis":"hint","ids":["in-3to5","in-tf-4"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"The Bennett angle is the setting he would leave off first; the SCI matters more","basis":"hint","ids":["in-bennett-off","in-bennett","in-sci-controls"]},{"lec":"instruments","t":"CR vs MIP mountings: a CR mounting finds MIP, an MIP mounting can't find CR","basis":"slides","ids":["in-mip-limit","in-tf-2"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Existing interferences: equal in symptomatic and asymptomatic patients; prophylactic equilibration not supported","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-interf-equal","mo-prophy","mo-tf-4","mo-2s-4","mo-slide2"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Introducing an interference (the high crown) causes protective co-contraction","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-cocontract","mo-cocontract-path","mo-new-medio","cs-mo-high-dx","cs-mo-high-tx"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Bruxism is centrally mediated, not started by occlusion; the splint protects the teeth","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-brux-tx","mo-brux-return","mo-num-6","cs-mo-brux-ask"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"What a TMD is: a collective term, muscles ~50%, joints ~35%, not orofacial pain","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-tmd-def","mo-tmd-split","mo-2s-1","mo-num-1"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Orthopedic instability: ~10% coincident, > 3 mm raises risk, loading (bruxism) adds to it","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-ortho-instab","mo-risk-mm","mo-risk-load","mo-2s-3","mo-tf-3"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Conservative first: reversible and irreversible treatments have similar success (50-90%)","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-success","mo-philosophy","mo-2s-6","mo-irrev-when"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Splints: stabilizing vs repositioning, maxillary vs mandibular, full coverage","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-stab-vs-repos","mo-max-splint","mo-partial","mo-num-3"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"The functional disturbance model and the five etiologies","basis":"slides","ids":["mo-five","mo-tf-2","mo-tuesday","mo-cause"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Tooth mobility: 28 µm, 150 µm limit, adapted vs traumatic, Miller's index","basis":"slides","ids":["mo-vert-28","mo-horiz","mo-miller2","mo-num-4","cs-mo-mob-type"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Mechanisms of wear and the erosion patterns (GERD, bulimia)","basis":"slides","ids":["mo-proud","mo-gerd","mo-num-5","cs-mo-eros-dx"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Muscles vs joints: movement problems in the muscles, loading problems in the joints","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-muscle-joint","mo-manns","mo-emg-medio"]},{"lec":"malocclusion","t":"Abfraction and noncarious cervical lesions: unproven clinically, multifactorial","basis":"hint","ids":["mo-abfraction","mo-nccl-site","mo-ncc-etio","mo-nccl-load"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Support, stability and retention: what each resists, and stability matters most","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-support","dn-ssr-most","dn-2s-1","dn-2s-8","cs-dn-flat-prop","cs-dn-ret-pb","dn-support-occl"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"The goal: keep the denture in the mouth; balance means resisting tipping","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-goal","dn-balance","dn-one-tooth"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Balanced vs non-balanced: contacts, anterior contact, teeth, articulator (his table)","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-bal-def","dn-nonbal-def","dn-2s-4","dn-num-3","dn-cg0"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Muscle control is the most important factor in keeping a denture in","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-tf-1","dn-fit","dn-num-1","dn-ret-fluid"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Resorption patterns, crossbite and pseudo-Class III","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-resorb-maxpost","dn-resorb-mand","dn-2s-3","dn-num-2","cs-dn-flat-rel"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"The maxillary anterior mold sets the rest, and it's chosen on esthetics","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-mold","dn-mold-basis","dn-berry","dn-md-length"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Lingualized occlusion: easy to set, balanced or not, no anterior contact","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-ling-pop","dn-ling-ant","dn-num-5","dn-sel-ling","dn-sel-lingadv"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Centric occlusion, not MIP, in a denture; CR is easy to find","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-cr","dn-2s-2"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Build and check the occlusion on an articulator: Hanau's Realeff","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-articulator","cs-dn-bal-marks","dn-tf-5","dn-hanau-m1"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Partial dentures: the more natural teeth remain, the more dentate the occlusion","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-partial","dn-tf-10"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"All removable is movable: what resists tipping, the tongue, interarch space","basis":"hint","ids":["dn-tf-3","dn-pps","dn-tongue","dn-recent","dn-tip-except"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"The four schemes compared: the slides' summary table","basis":"slides","ids":["dn-compare-adjust","dn-compare-stab","dn-tf-12"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Non-balanced is neutrocentric: neutralize the inclines, centralize the forces","basis":"slides","ids":["dn-neutro-40","dn-num-7","dn-m2"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Tooth forms (30-33°, 12-22°, 0°), their names, for and against, and choosing for the patient","basis":"slides","ids":["dn-anat-angle","dn-flat-pro","dn-evidence","cs-dn-flat-scheme","cs-dn-bal-scheme","dn-tf-8","dn-2s-9","dn-tf-13","dn-tf-14"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Hanau and Thielemann: the five determinants, which are fixed and which vary","basis":"slides","ids":["dn-quint","dn-reverse","dn-fixed-po","dn-2s-10"]},{"lec":"dentures","t":"Denture tooth materials: porcelain, composite and cross-linked resin, acrylic","basis":"slides","ids":["dn-porc","dn-comp","dn-acrylic","dn-num-4","dn-wear","dn-reverse-curve"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"Three causes, alone or together: abrasion, abfraction and erosion; carious vs noncarious","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-three","nc-def","nc-2s-1","nc-rmgi"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"Abrasion: the toothpaste matters more than the brush; RDA (higher is more abrasive)","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-rda","nc-2s-2","cs-nc-br-first"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"Not every NCCL is from brushing: leprosy, archaeological, animals, the palate, subgingival","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-num-1","nc-sel-evid","nc-tf-1"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"Abfraction: an inclined-plane load, weaker in tension, the lesion just above the fulcrum","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-tension","nc-num-2","nc-wf-1"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"pH vs buffering capacity: high is bad; orange juice and wine; citric acid chelates calcium","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-buffer-def","nc-citric","nc-2s-4","nc-num-3","cs-nc-ac-drink"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"Match the treatment to the cause (his last slide)","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-why-etio","nc-sensor","nc-sel-acid","nc-match-fix","nc-tf-4","cs-nc-gr-tx"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"When to restore: cleansable, doesn't hurt, doesn't look bad, not deep","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-restore","nc-tf-8","cs-nc-br-restore","cs-nc-gr-restore","cs-nc-ac-6"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"The clues: dominant side for brushing; tori and wear facets for abfraction","basis":"lecture","ids":["nc-hand","nc-tf-3","nc-tf-5","cs-nc-br-cause","cs-nc-gr-cause"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"Erosion vs caries, and where vomiting erodes","basis":"lecture","ids":["nc-caries-sites","nc-ws-2","nc-tf-9","nc-2s-5","cs-nc-ac-cause"]},{"lec":"nccl","t":"Water dilutes, bicarbonate neutralizes (pH is a log scale)","basis":"hint","ids":["nc-tf-6","cs-nc-ac-help"]}],"examCounts":{},"mock":{"blueprint":{"instruments":12,"malocclusion":12,"dentures":12,"nccl":9},"limit":3600},"srcLabel":{"handout":"Slides & handout","hub":"From the recordings","help":"Dr. Givan's help sheet"},"drillHy":{"help":"built on Dr. Givan's \"What cusp hits where?\" help sheet"},"achievements":[{"id":"first","name":"First Contact","icon":"tooth","desc":"Answer your first question","cond":{"type":"totalCorrect","n":1}},{"id":"fifty","name":"Fifty Strokes","icon":"cycle","desc":"50 correct answers","cond":{"type":"totalCorrect","n":50}},{"id":"streak10","name":"Canine Guided","icon":"target","desc":"10 correct in a row","cond":{"type":"streak","n":10}},{"id":"streak25","name":"Mutually Protected","icon":"shield","desc":"25 correct in a row","cond":{"type":"streak","n":25}},{